tasks.description:
Fixes review findings P106-R12 to P106-R17. The phase lock must name a live process for as long as a session holds the phase.

- A keep start runs _take_phase_lock as a take does, so another run's live lock refuses it. When the ours branch finds a recorded pid that is gone, or one that differs from the current process, it re-takes the lock for this run so the lock names the live process. _locks.held_by_us applies the same refresh on a same-session re-entry whose recorded pid is gone, so acquire no longer answers 'already yours' over a lock every other reader judges dead.
- The orchestrator's acquire can meet a start-noted lock of its own session. It then re-takes that lock under its own note, so sign-off's start-note release leaves it in place and the merge runs under a held lock.
- When the releasing session never held the lock and the holder is live, _release_start_lock prints its own wording: it names the live holder and says the lock was left in place. release_refusal's takeover text stays for a run that actually held the lock.
- The span from the lock take to the validated write is covered: an exception there runs undo_lock and re-raises.
- _locks exposes one public predicate for "the holder is gone". judge, the release conflict and audit-task's _holder_gone all use it, and no private _locks helper is called from another module.

Cases. Each refusal gets an allow twin, driven in scratch repos with distinct session ids and pids:
- a same-session start under a new process after the old one died, followed by another session's start, is refused;
- a keep start while another session's live lock is held is refused;
- hand start, then orchestrator acquire, then sign-off: the lock is still held;
- a sign-off by a non-holder while the holder is live prints the left-in-place wording;
- an exception injected between take and write leaves no lock.
